es the community’s overall structure: measurement (PSA test) → output (PSA level) → interpretation influenced by multiple modifying factors. [Data: Entities (3214, 3251, 3213); Relationships (6448, 6852)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Elevated PSA is explicitly treated as a warning sign, not a definitive diagnosis\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Within the community, a high PSA level is characterized as a “warning sign” that may require further testing rather than automatically indicating cancer. This directly shapes how decision-makers and clinicians should treat abnormal values—triggering additional evaluation rather than concluding disease from the PSA result alone. [Data: Entities (3236); Relationships (6414)]\\n\\nThe PSA test and PSA level descriptions reinforce this non-definitive framing by noting that PSA can be elevated for reasons other than cancer and that interpretation varies between individuals and contexts. This emphasizes that the community’s core risk is interpretive error (over- or under-attribution of meaning to PSA elevation) rather than any single entity’s behavior. [Data: Entities (3214, 3251)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Non-cancer causes and confounders of PSA elevation are central to the community (UTI and enlarged prostate)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Urinary tract infection (UTI) is repeatedly identified as a factor that can raise PSA levels and complicate PSA test interpretation. The data also explicitly state that an elevated PSA in the presence of UTI does not automatically indicate prostate cancer and may warrant reassessment after the infection is addressed. [Data: Entities (3226, 3251); Relationships (6412)]\\n\\nEnlarged prostate is likewise described as a noncancerous condition that can increase PSA production and lead to elevated PSA levels, making PSA results less specific for prostate cancer. The community also notes that health issues besides cancer can cause an enlarged prostate, further underscoring that PSA elevation has multiple plausible non-malignant explanations. [Data: Entities (3225, 3361); Relationships (6669)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Transient behavioral effects (sex/exercise and vigorous exercise) are recognized and managed via temporary avoidance before testing\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes behavioral factors that can transiently increase PSA. Sex and exercise are described as behaviors that can raise PSA temporarily, prompting clinicians to recommend temporary avoidance before PSA testing to reduce confounding. [Data: Entities (3352); Relationships (6613)]\\n\\nVigorous exercise is also specifically linked to transient PSA increases, reinforcing that timing and pre-test behavior can materially affect PSA level interpretation. Together, these relationships indicate a practical operational control in the community: pre-test guidance designed to improve the interpretability of PSA results. [Data: Entities (3362, 3251); Relationships (6654)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Medications, herbals, and supplements are explicitly flagged as interpretive modifiers of PSA level\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community notes that drugs and supplements (including medicines, herbals, and supplements) can affect PSA level and complicate clinical interpretation. This introduces a medication/supplement history as a relevant contextual input when evaluating PSA results. [Data: Entities (3353, 3251); Relationships (6647)]\\n\\nBecause PSA level is used to guide next steps in assessment, the presence of medication-related effects increases the importance of documenting exposures and considering them when interpreting changes over time or single elevated results. The dataset does not quantify effect sizes, but it clearly establishes the directionally important concept of pharmacologic and supplement confounding. [Data: Entities (3251, 3353); Relationships (6647)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Standardized measurement units